.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt RC 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm rc ,
.Nm rc.shutdown ,
.Nm rc.d
.Nd system startup and shutdown scripts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /sbin/init_sh /etc/rc Op Cm autoboot
.Nm /sbin/init_sh /etc/rc.shutdown
.Nm /etc/rc.d/ Ns Ar service Ar command
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /etc/rc
starts the system's services when it comes up to multi-user mode.
.Xr init 8
runs it with the argument
.Cm autoboot
when the system boots unattended.
It loads the settings in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
orders the scripts in
.Pa /etc/rc.d
and
.Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d
with
.Xr rcorder 8 ,
leaving out those with the keyword
.Cm nostart ,
and runs each with the command
.Cm faststart
.Po
.Cm quietstart
when not booting
.Pc .
A script that fails is reported on the console and the boot continues; only
a failure of
.Pa /etc/rc
itself makes
.Xr init 8
fall back to single-user mode.
.Pp
.Pa /etc/rc.shutdown
stops services before the system goes down.
It runs the scripts that have the keyword
.Cm shutdown ,
in the reverse of their start order, with the command
.Cm faststop .
.Xr init 8
stops it after
.Va rcshutdown_timeout
seconds.
.Pp
Each script in
.Pa /etc/rc.d
manages one service, and is run by
.Xr init_sh 8 .
It may be written with the
.Xr rc.subr 8
functions, in the style of
.Fx
scripts, or as an
.Xr init_sh 8
service block.
Its commands are
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm start
Start the service, if it is enabled in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.It Cm stop
Stop the service.
.It Cm restart
Stop the service, then start it.
.It Cm status
Report whether the service is running; exit 0 if it is.
.It Cm rcvar
Print the
.Xr rc.conf 5
variable that enables the service, and its value.
.It Cm enabled
Exit 0 if the service is enabled.
.El
.Pp
A script may define more commands.
A command may be prefixed with
.Cm one ,
to run it even if the service is disabled;
.Cm force ,
to also ignore failed preconditions;
.Cm quiet ,
to omit some messages; or
.Cm fast ,
to skip the check for an already running service.
.Pp
The scripts
.Pa FILESYSTEMS ,
.Pa NETWORKING ,
.Pa SERVERS ,
.Pa DAEMON
and
.Pa LOGIN
do nothing; they provide points in the boot that other scripts can require.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/defaults/rc.conf -compact
.It Pa /etc/rc
the startup script
.It Pa /etc/rc.shutdown
the shutdown script
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/
the base system's service scripts
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d/
service scripts for added software
.It Pa /etc/rc.conf
settings; see
.Xr rc.conf 5
.It Pa /etc/defaults/rc.conf
default settings
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/rc.d/hostname status
# /etc/rc.d/cron onestart
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr ttys 5 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr init_sh 8 ,
.Xr rc.subr 8 ,
.Xr rcorder 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
script appeared in
.At v6 .
The
.Pa rc.d
system first appeared in
.Nx 1.5 .
